{"subjectId":"python","id":"decorators","title":"Decorators","group":"Functions","order":29,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A decorator takes a function and returns a new function that wraps it — adding behaviour like timing, logging, caching or access checks without touching the original code.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>`@decorator`</strong> — syntax sugar: <code>@timer</code> above <code>def f</code> means <code>f = timer(f)</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Writing one</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef shout(func):\n    @functools.wraps(func)            # keep func's name and docstring\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        return result.upper()\n    return wrapper\n\n@shout\ndef greet(name):\n    \"\"\"Say hello.\"\"\"\n    return f\"hello, {name}\"\n\nprint(greet(\"asha\"))                     # HELLO, ASHA\nprint(greet.__name__, greet.__doc__)     # greet Say hello.</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Without <code>functools.wraps</code>, the decorated function reports the name <code>wrapper</code> and loses its docstring — confusing in debugging and documentation.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Decorators with arguments</h3>\n<p>Add one more level: a function that takes the arguments and <em>returns</em> the decorator.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef retry(times):\n    def decorator(func):\n        @functools.wraps(func)\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            for attempt in range(1, times + 1):\n                try:\n                    return func(*args, **kwargs)\n                except ValueError:\n                    if attempt == times:\n                        raise\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\ncalls = {\"n\": 0}\n\n@retry(times=3)\ndef flaky():\n    calls[\"n\"] += 1\n    if calls[\"n\"] &lt; 3:\n        raise ValueError(\"not yet\")\n    return \"ok\"\n\nprint(flaky(), calls[\"n\"])   # ok 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>A timing decorator</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools, time\n\ndef timed(func):\n    @functools.wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            return func(*args, **kwargs)\n        finally:\n            elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n            wrapper.last_ms = round(elapsed * 1000, 3)\n    return wrapper\n\n@timed\ndef work():\n    return sum(range(100_000))\n\nprint(work())                            # 4999950000\nprint(isinstance(work.last_ms, float))   # True</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack\"></i>Stacking &amp; built-in decorators</h3>\n<p>Stacked decorators apply bottom-up: <code>@a</code> over <code>@b</code> over <code>def f</code> means <code>f = a(b(f))</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Decorator</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>@property</code></td><td>Expose a method as a read-only (or validated) attribute</td></tr><tr><td><code>@classmethod</code> / <code>@staticmethod</code></td><td>Methods that receive the class / nothing instead of <code>self</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>@functools.cache</code> / <code>lru_cache</code></td><td>Memoization</td></tr><tr><td><code>@dataclasses.dataclass</code></td><td>Generate <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code>, <code>__eq__</code> for a class</td></tr><tr><td><code>@contextlib.contextmanager</code></td><td>Turn a generator into a <code>with</code>-statement context manager</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>"}